 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A habitat is the place where an organism lives, for example a forest, a river, a savanna or the soil.\nThe environment is everything around an organism. Environmental factors affect what can live in a place:\n• climatic factors (weather and climate): temperature, rainfall, light, humidity and wind;\n• edaphic factors (soil): texture (sand, clay, loam), the amount of water and air in the soil, minerals and humus, and acidity (pH);\n• biotic factors: the other living things (food, predators, competitors, people).\nExample: the rainforest of the South has heavy rain, warmth and deep, humus-rich soil; the savanna of the North has a long dry season, so grasses and drought-tolerant trees grow there.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why do mangroves grow along the coast near Douala and Limbe, but not on the dry plains of the Far North?",
  "steps": [
   "Mangroves need salty, waterlogged mud and a warm, wet climate.",
   "The coast has these edaphic and climatic factors; the Far North is dry."
  ],
  "answer": "Only the coast gives the wet, salty soil and climate they need.",
  "checks": []
 },
